=========================================================
JavaScript, Browser & Web APIs
========================================================= 

JavaScript is a programming language.
JavaScript itself has NO access to browser features.

To run JavaScript, we need a runtime environment.
In the browser, that runtime is the JavaScript Engine.


=========================================================
JavaScript Engine
========================================================= 

The browser contains a JavaScript Engine (like V8).

The JavaScript Engine has:
1) Call Stack  → Executes JavaScript code
2) Heap        → Stores memory (objects, variables)

=========================================================
How JavaScript Engine Executes Code ( Call Stack Notes )
========================================================= 

• JavaScript is single-threaded, meaning it executes one task at a time.
• The JavaScript engine uses a Call Stack to manage execution of code.
• Call Stack follows LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle.

Call Stack Basics:
• A stack data structure that stores execution contexts.
• Keeps track of which function is currently running.

Execution Flow:
• When the program starts, the Global Execution Context (GEC) is created and pushed to the stack.
• JavaScript executes code line by line inside the GEC.
• When a function is called, a new Function Execution Context is created.
• This function context is pushed onto the call stack.
• When the function finishes execution, it is popped off the stack.
• Control returns to the previous execution context.
• When all code finishes, the call stack becomes empty.

Example Call Order:
Global → function A → function B → function B ends → function A ends → Global ends

Important Points:
• Only one function can execute at a time.
• Too many nested calls or infinite recursion causes stack overflow.
• The call stack handles only synchronous code.
• Asynchronous tasks are handled by Web APIs and the Event Loop.

One-line Summary:
• The call stack ensures JavaScript executes functions in the correct order using LIFO. 



================================================================
The browser has many powers that JavaScript alone does NOT have
================================================================
Web APIs (provided by the browser, not JavaScript itself)
================================================================

Web APIs 
they are provided by the browser and expose their functionality
to the JavaScript engine, allowing JavaScript code (executed in the
Call Stack) to interact with browser features.

Note:
Web APIs are exposed to JavaScript through the global `window` object.
Thats why methods like `setTimeout()` are actually `window.setTimeout()`.
We usually dont write `window.` because JavaScript automatically looks
for these methods on the `window` object by default.

Web APIs 
- DOM APIs → DOM access
  (document, querySelector, getElementById, etc.)

- Timer APIs → Timers
  (setTimeout(), setInterval())

- Network APIs → Network requests (HTTP/HTTPS)
  (fetch(), XMLHttpRequest)

- Storage APIs → Storage
  (localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB)

- Console API → Console
  (console.log(), console.error(), etc.)

- Location & Device APIs → Location, Bluetooth, Camera, etc.
  (location, geolocation, MediaDevices, Bluetooth)

Important:
- setTimeout(), fetch(), localStorage, console.log(), location
  are NOT JavaScript language features.
- They are Web APIs provided by the browser.
- JavaScript can only call them; the browser executes them.

One-line summary:
JavaScript runs the logic.
Web APIs provide extra capabilities.
The browser owns and executes those capabilities.

=========================================================
JavaScript Event Loop ( Step-by-Step )
=========================================================
Code:
console.log("start");

setTimeout(function cb() {
  console.log("callback");
}, 5000);

console.log("End");


Execution Steps:
1) console.log("start")
- Goes to Call Stack
- Executes immediately
- Output: start
- Removed from stack

2) setTimeout(cb, 5000)
- Goes to Call Stack
- Browser registers timer in Web APIs
- Callback is NOT executed now
- setTimeout is removed from stack

3) console.log("End")
- Goes to Call Stack
- Executes immediately
- Output: End
- Removed from stack

4) After 5 seconds
- Timer finishes in Web APIs
- Callback (cb) is pushed into Callback Queue

5) Event Loop
- Continuously checks Call Stack
- If Call Stack is empty → moves callback from
  Callback Queue to Call Stack

6) Callback execution
- console.log("callback") runs
- Output: callback

Final Output Order:
start
End
callback

Key Rules:
- JavaScript is single-threaded
- setTimeout is non-blocking
- setTimeout delay is minimum, not exact
- Event Loop runs callbacks only when Call Stack is empty